{
  "esm": "2.3.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "ppmflux_D_lon_inflow_east",
    "description": "The upwind-biased, monotone, conservative flux-form zonal advection D(U*q) over the three columns next to the east wall (i = NLON-2..NLON) of the regional latlon3d grid at once, OPEN walls included: exactly the expression of ./ppmflux_D_lon_interior (the same spherical metric and the same two face fluxes ../stencils/ppmflux_lev_face_flux, upper face first), with each window member of a face read through the lateral halo. The upper face of cell i reads cells i-2..i+3 and U at face i+1, the lower face cells i-3..i+2 and U at face i. A member outside the domain is the prescribed lateral-boundary concentration (qbc_w below cell 1, qbc_e above cell NLON), so each read is ifelse(inside, q at the clamped cell, halo): the subscript stays in range and the halo is selected exactly. This is the boundary closure the open-wall rule has always used -- every near-wall slab of the earlier spelling read exactly this window -- written so that the lon index is an ordinary array axis and a whole three-column slab is one aggregate instead of three one-column stencils. At q == 1 with the halo == 1 every slope is 0.0 and each face flux is the wind bitwise. Only the east wall's closure can act on these columns (requires NLON >= 6), so only that side's reads carry it; the window members toward the other wall are read by plain offsets. The interior columns use ./ppmflux_D_lon_interior and the opposite slab ./ppmflux_D_lon_inflow_west.",
    "license": "MIT",
    "tags": [
      "esd:stencil",
      "family:finite_volume",
      "grid:latlon3d",
      "op:D",
      "axes:lon,lat,lev",
      "order:3",
      "scheme:upwind",
      "stencil_width:7"
    ],
    "references": [
      {
        "citation": "Colella, P., & Woodward, P. R. (1984). The Piecewise Parabolic Method (PPM) for gas-dynamical simulations. Journal of Computational Physics, 54(1), 174-201.",
        "doi": "10.1016/0021-9991(84)90143-8"
      },
      {
        "citation": "Lin, S.-J., & Rood, R. B. (1996). Multidimensional flux-form semi-Lagrangian transport schemes. Monthly Weather Review, 124(9), 2046-2070.",
        "doi": "10.1175/1520-0493(1996)124<2046:MFFSLT>2.0.CO;2"
      },
      {
        "citation": "Williamson, D. L., Drake, J. B., Hack, J. J., Jakob, R., & Swarztrauber, P. N. (1992). A standard test set for numerical approximations to the shallow water equations in spherical geometry. Journal of Computational Physics, 102(1), 211-224. (Spherical lat-lon metric factors for the zonal flux divergence.)",
        "doi": "10.1016/S0021-9991(05)80016-6"
      }
    ]
  },
  "expression_template_imports": [
    {
      "ref": "../grid.esm"
    },
    {
      "ref": "./ppmflux_lev_face_flux.esm"
    }
  ],
